eorem states the following: \n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-3)}{3+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-125248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-1"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{16}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-73354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/029a51dccba9f1ec927c820fb8adb72e926b88a881f4646090ac651ef29da271.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on $(-5,3).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-73354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{3+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-73354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-73354" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-73354" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{5}{8}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{8}{3}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{8}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-73354" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)=-3\\sqrt{x}+2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\sqrt{2}+2)-(-3\\sqrt{0}+2)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\sqrt{x}+2)=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-2344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "1\\,,\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\pm \\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+x-2$ on the interval $[-1,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,2)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(-1)}{2-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+2-2)-(3\\cdot (-1)^{2}+(-1)-2)}{2+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+x-2)=6x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=6c+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6c+1 & =4 \\\\ 6c & =3 \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2305" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-2305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2305" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)={x}^{2}$ on the interval $[-8,6].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-8,6]$ and differentiable on $(-8,6).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-8,6)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-8$ and $b=6$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(6)-f(-8)}{6-(-8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}-(-8)^{2}}{14} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =-2 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2341" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-2341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-1\\,,2"] ["math" "-2\\,,1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,,-2"] ["math" "\\pm 1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2341" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x+5$ on the interval $[1,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ and differentiable on $(1,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (1,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{({4}^{2}-2\\cdot 4+5)-({1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+5)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-4}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2x+5)=2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c-2 & =3 \\\\ 2c & =5 \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-2342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}\\,"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}\\,,5"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}\\,"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\pm 5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-2342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x)=5\\sqrt{x}-2$ on the interval $[4,9].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,9]$ and differentiable on $(4,9),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,9)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(9)-f(4)}{9-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\sqrt{9}-2)-(5\\sqrt{4}-2)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-8}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-2)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ c & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{25}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=2.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-77888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/266cc36afab26e5af24044f66b8689b3abc550409d40336375e896064b1badf3.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/49db2f4f9b88672a0d3a8229f80850b6187f2cebc59a8a334f869d3ffa7a56a6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/535745a110dcf5c361965127a8d25455687415c547f8ee7406d0add33121589b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/db518cdc174cb245f2479f44a185266f274d9c6f2b5ef90f3d752aadf761726d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ef3499bbe65662a44c92b3c5ce5023bfa74b8cbd2f37dc182bdc62dbcffba1f3.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/db518cdc174cb245f2479f44a185266f274d9c6f2b5ef90f3d752aadf761726d.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $s(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/09017ce160ebb2995e0c7c4efe81fd5444ba8cedd91dc270c69757b8b0eb1ae4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/45db5f9f600f29e91fb40335f80fb28bb12f87fc4618c623354b91408f6bb09c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/c4c45b3d88dd9821c1c5650e898d806b8887838c609548296040feae8915e40b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e50696609559e7996dda87b3566639e0f1772b0538b9b41e8311cb49b5c41a5b.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $t(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a cusp at $x=1.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-48399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/36a329b531e3d48d82585ee336b10dff8b4b78585829a56d72cdac61e0aba45d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/66663e046f3c00d3f4884b7129cdc5b5d4be18574c6f2fc47fd39dcc976e398c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/78a7985112e69fa2a647ad4548791ef42bee13c458d1e160199f767771315513.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e6ccc7f465d50c13059b93e1b12b80bc3c10b9970ea0fedd5a3b3b9d4d3cabf5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/fab6a73c970500b896e38e75faf21c1fd5aecf9909d9537963e269fd5f60c70d.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e6ccc7f465d50c13059b93e1b12b80bc3c10b9970ea0fedd5a3b3b9d4d3cabf5.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=2.$  Hence, $g(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, $q(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has corner points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-77875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" 