er/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(1,4).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Hence, $h(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a cusp at $x=2.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/0680d1da0bbcc0d40668bef248d55441acd77c1304545d6710256eb023023b05.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6283b99ea5ee48cf8dbbf56297a03d29e7de3a09ed3fdeac9376b3a875d045d6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6cd605b5533bf0211bf75d3cc1d461fe41ee382e8799bd2337c4a53627eb0ca9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b629cd9686bb3f9b671a9412552ccd4161cb832d8e8426ee83a8955b81cc5831.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ec5215bf37cce6a5b6a6f6cddcc6366e757ae464bdbde83c7276ca2db85d64cd.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ec5215bf37cce6a5b6a6f6cddcc6366e757ae464bdbde83c7276ca2db85d64cd.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $f(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ but has a corner at $x=1.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{4}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ is not defined on $(-\\infty ,1).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function II satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e]?$\n\n- $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\n- $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous at $x=0,$ which means it is not continuous on $[0,e].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125262" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-125262" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125262" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-125262" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $h(x)=2x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-48499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-48499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\n- $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$\n- $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x-1|)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function I satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85"} {"question" "q-127014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "q-127014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "2bf4e31658487e701286eb1356841947da7476ff767ec57b58ef339ce9f53d1c"} {"question" "e-3718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3718.json" "file_sha256" "b848f56c46eca570a8494b5a1f06ca9c9c21e811716f35b79eb4ff20a4a400d4"} {"question" "e-3718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3718.json" "file_sha256" "b848f56c46eca570a8494b5a1f06ca9c9c21e811716f35b79eb4ff20a4a400d4"} {"question" "e-3717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3717.json" "file_sha256" "9c02721c0969f0299723ac1dce6b7790f9a64332ef10c68c8c6c2cb8e6933c64"} {"question" "e-3717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3717.json" "file_sha256" "9c02721c0969f0299723ac1dce6b7790f9a64332ef10c68c8c6c2cb8e6933c64"} {"question" "e-3719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3719.json" "file_sha256" "85619a0b932b3fdecf155ade34ce6e96a9d1eb8014375f28521dab320577d45d"} {"question" "e-3719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3719.json" "file_sha256" "85619a0b932b3fdecf155ade34ce6e96a9d1eb8014375f28521dab320577d45d"} {"question" "e-6738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-6738.json" "file_sha256" "6f11550634d3d4dd82ad314aa8b38472afd2df84c81c8b3dbb07050a0ad37375"} {"question" "e-6738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-6738.json" "file_sha256" "6f11550634d3d4dd82ad314aa8b38472afd2df84c81c8b3dbb07050a0ad37375"}] "usage" {"e-3719" [] "e-3717" [] "q-48499" [] "e-3718" [] "q-2344" [] "q-2341" [] "e-6738" []} "basis" 1531 "retractions" [[17592186064541 :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n\n$f(x) =-3\\sqrt{x} + 2$ on the interval $[0, 2]$."] [17592186064540 :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n\n$f(x) = x^{2}$ on the interval $[- 8, 6]$."] [17592186064736 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[- 1, 1]$?\n\n1. $g(x) = \\mid x \\mid$\n2. $s(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$\n3. $h(x) = 2x$"] [17592186063959 :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$. Complete the sentence below.\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\,$.*\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/50f7d14f01067a1e593877f08d34b5b2.png)"] [17592186063959 :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n> *There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n> $f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}$.*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4$, so the above statement becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(4) - f(-1))/(4 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 - 2}{4 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}$."] [17592186063958 :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2]$."] [17592186063958 :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$.\n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\nf'(c) = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(2) - f(0))/(2 - (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{2} - 0^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) = 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c &= 2 \\\\\nc &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186063960 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/412e4f68cffe765264fae545418ef7fe.png)"] [17592186063960 :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$, the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2)$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2$. So it is not continuous on $[-2,2]$, and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2$, it is right-continuous at $x=-2$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0$. So, it is not differentiable at $x=0$, which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2)$. Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$."] [17592186064036 :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$?\n\n1. $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n2. $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n3. $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$"] [17592186064036 :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$, the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0$, hence, it is not continuous at\n$x = 0$, and so it is not continuous on $[0,1]$. Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186061757 17592186061758 17592186061759 17592186061760 17592186061761 17592186061763 17592186061764 17592186061765 17592186061766 17592186061767 17592186062928 17592186062929 17592186062930 17592186062931 17592186062932] "immutable_fields" [[17592186061756 :answer-field/correct] [17592186061756 :answer-field/id] [17592186061756 :answer-field/key] [17592186061756 :answer-field/type] [17592186061762 :answer-field/correct] [17592186061762 :answer-field/id] [17592186061762 :answer-field/key] [17592186061762 :answer-field/type] [17592186062927 :answer-field/correct] [17592186062927 :answer-field/id] [17592186062927 :answer-field/key] [17592186062927 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
